## Linux DRM - GEM: handle, dumb buffer, mmap
```require
- Kernel: Based on Linux v6.19 source
- Prerequisites: Linux DRM Part 2, Part 3 open() and ioctl()
```
### Introduction
| Part | Topic |
|---|---|
| Part 1 | <a href="/posts/content/2026-10-26-post-linux-drm-01-architecture">Architecture and the Big Picture</a> |
| Part 2 | <a href="/posts/content/2026-10-27-post-linux-drm-02-register">Core Initialization and Driver Registration</a> |
| Part 3 | <a href="/posts/content/2026-10-28-post-linux-drm-03-open-ioctl">open() and ioctl() Code Flow</a> |

In Parts 1 through 3, we examined the DRM architecture and the `open`/`ioctl` flow, noting that <mark>object_idr</mark> inside `struct drm_file` serves as the GEM handle table. This post begins from there.  

| Question | Code Examined in This Post |
|---|---|
| Who creates and references the buffers used by the GPU? | GEM (`drm_gem_object`, handle) |
| How is the simplest buffer created? | Dumb buffer creation (`DRM_IOCTL_MODE_CREATE_DUMB`) |
| How does the CPU read and write to that buffer? | `mmap`, fake offset, page fault |


```adcard
```

### Why Is Memory Management DRM's Job
Because the GPU is a separate processor from the CPU, rendering graphics requires both the CPU and GPU to manage <b>the same memory together</b>. Textures, framebuffers, and command buffers all fall into this category. This presents challenges that cannot be solved with standard `malloc`.  

- To allow GPU access, memory must be <b>pinned to physical pages</b> and mapped into a GPU-accessible address space (via an IOMMU or the GPU's own page table).
- When a process terminates, the GPU memory it occupied must be <b>automatically reclaimed</b>.
- <b>Different processes must view the same buffer</b>, such as a display server and a client application.
- <b>Synchronization</b> is required so that the CPU does not modify a buffer while the GPU is actively accessing it.

DRM solves this problem through an interface called <b>GEM (Graphics Execution Manager)</b>. It represents every GPU buffer as a single `struct drm_gem_object`, while a <b>backend (helper)</b> determines where the actual memory is sourced. Representative examples include `drm_gem_shmem_helper.c`, which uses main memory shmem; `drm_gem_dma_helper.c`, which uses contiguous memory; and TTM-based `drm_gem_ttm_helper.c`, which manages dedicated GPU memory (VRAM) as well. The code in this post follows the simplest <b>shmem backend</b> (used by drivers like vkms).  

### The Core of GEM: drm_gem_object and Handles
#### The Object: struct drm_gem_object
```c
{
    "title": "include/drm/drm_gem.h - struct drm_gem_object (L280-455)"
}
---
struct drm_gem_object {
	struct kref refcount;
	unsigned handle_count;  // [!code highlight]
	struct drm_device *dev;
	struct file *filp;  // [!code highlight]
	struct drm_vma_offset_node vma_node;  // [!code highlight]
	size_t size;
	int name;
	struct dma_buf *dma_buf;  // [!code highlight]
	struct dma_buf_attachment *import_attach;  // [!code highlight]
	struct dma_resv *resv;  // [!code highlight]
	struct dma_resv _resv;  // [!code highlight]
	struct {
		struct list_head list;
		struct mutex lock;
	} gpuva;
	const struct drm_gem_object_funcs *funcs;  // [!code highlight]
	struct list_head lru_node;
	struct drm_gem_lru *lru;
};
```

> **Source** <a href="https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/v6.19/include/drm/drm_gem.h#L280-L455" target="_blank">include/drm/drm_gem.h L280-L455</a> (Linux v6.19)

- <mark>funcs</mark>: The function table implemented by the backend (`free`, `mmap`, `vmap`, `export`, `get_sg_table`, ...). The core handles common tasks and delegates memory-type-specific logic here.
- <mark>filp</mark>: In the shmem backend, this is the shmem file holding the actual backing memory of the object.
- <mark>vma_node</mark>: Holds the "fake offset" used to locate the object during `mmap` (covered in the mmap section below).
- <mark>dma_buf</mark>, `import_attach`: The dma-buf connection used when sharing buffers with other devices (covered in the PRIME section).
- <mark>resv</mark>: The `dma_resv` that aggregates fences attached to this buffer (covered in the synchronization section).

```c
{
    "title": "include/drm/drm_gem.h - struct drm_gem_object_funcs (L72-235)"
}
---
struct drm_gem_object_funcs {
	void (*free)(struct drm_gem_object *obj);
	int (*open)(struct drm_gem_object *obj, struct drm_file *file);
	void (*close)(struct drm_gem_object *obj, struct drm_file *file);
	void (*print_info)(struct drm_printer *p, unsigned int indent,
			   const struct drm_gem_object *obj);
	struct dma_buf *(*export)(struct drm_gem_object *obj, int flags);
	int (*pin)(struct drm_gem_object *obj);
	void (*unpin)(struct drm_gem_object *obj);
	struct sg_table *(*get_sg_table)(struct drm_gem_object *obj);
	int (*vmap)(struct drm_gem_object *obj, struct iosys_map *map);
	void (*vunmap)(struct drm_gem_object *obj, struct iosys_map *map);
	int (*mmap)(struct drm_gem_object *obj, struct vm_area_struct *vma);
	int (*evict)(struct drm_gem_object *obj);
	enum drm_gem_object_status (*status)(struct drm_gem_object *obj);
	size_t (*rss)(struct drm_gem_object *obj);
	const struct vm_operations_struct *vm_ops;
};
```

> **Source** <a href="https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/v6.19/include/drm/drm_gem.h#L72-L235" target="_blank">include/drm/drm_gem.h L72-L235</a> (Linux v6.19)

#### The Name Tag: Handles Are per fd
Userspace cannot directly know the object's kernel pointer. Instead, it receives an integer <b>handle</b>. This handle is an index into <mark>drm_file.object_idr</mark> introduced in Part 1. In other words, <b>handles reside in an independent namespace for each `open()`ed file descriptor (fd)</b>; even for the exact same object, different processes can have different handles.  

```mermaid
{
    "title": "Handles are per drm_file — different numbers can point to the same object"
}
---
flowchart LR
    subgraph PA["Process A's drm_file"]
        IA["object_idr<br/>1 → obj X<br/>2 → obj Y"]
    end
    subgraph PB["Process B's drm_file"]
        IB["object_idr<br/>1 → obj Y<br/>2 → obj Z"]
    end
    X["drm_gem_object X"]
    Y["drm_gem_object Y<br/>handle_count = 2"]
    Z["drm_gem_object Z"]
    IA -->|"handle 1"| X
    IA -->|"handle 2"| Y
    IB -->|"handle 1"| Y
    IB -->|"handle 2"| Z
```
This table is initialized during `open()` (in `drm_file_alloc`, covered in Part 3). This is also where handle numbering is configured to <b>start from 1</b>.  

```c
{
    "title": "drivers/gpu/drm/drm_gem.c - drm_gem_open() (L1027-1032)"
}
---
void
drm_gem_open(struct drm_device *dev, struct drm_file *file_private)
{
	idr_init_base(&file_private->object_idr, 1);  // [!code highlight]
	spin_lock_init(&file_private->table_lock);
}
```

> **Source** <a href="https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/v6.19/drivers/gpu/drm/drm_gem.c#L1027-L1032" target="_blank">drivers/gpu/drm/drm_gem.c L1027-L1032</a> (Linux v6.19)

The function that creates handles is `drm_gem_handle_create()`, while the actual work is performed by its tail function.  

```c
{
    "title": "drivers/gpu/drm/drm_gem.c - drm_gem_handle_create_tail() (L437-495)"
}
---
int
drm_gem_handle_create_tail(struct drm_file *file_priv,
			   struct drm_gem_object *obj,
			   u32 *handlep)
{
	/* ... */
	drm_gem_object_handle_get(obj);
	/* ... */
	idr_preload(GFP_KERNEL);
	spin_lock(&file_priv->table_lock);

	ret = idr_alloc(&file_priv->object_idr, NULL, 1, 0, GFP_NOWAIT);  // [!code highlight]

	spin_unlock(&file_priv->table_lock);
	idr_preload_end();
	/* ... */
	mutex_unlock(&dev->object_name_lock);
	/* ... */
	handle = ret;

	ret = drm_vma_node_allow(&obj->vma_node, file_priv);  // [!code highlight]
	if (ret)
		goto err_remove;

	if (obj->funcs->open) {  // [!code highlight]
		ret = obj->funcs->open(obj, file_priv);  // [!code highlight]
		if (ret)
			goto err_revoke;
	}

	spin_lock(&file_priv->table_lock);
	obj = idr_replace(&file_priv->object_idr, obj, handle);  // [!code highlight]
	WARN_ON(obj != NULL);
	spin_unlock(&file_priv->table_lock);
	*handlep = handle;
	/* ... */
}
```

> **Source** <a href="https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/v6.19/drivers/gpu/drm/drm_gem.c#L437-L495" target="_blank">drivers/gpu/drm/drm_gem.c L437-L495</a> (Linux v6.19)

- `idr_alloc(&file_priv->object_idr, NULL, 1, 0, ...)`: Allocates an <mark>available number >= 1</mark> from this file's IDR table. Initially, `NULL` is placed instead of the object pointer.
- `drm_vma_node_allow()`: Grants this file permission to `mmap` this object (checked later via `drm_vma_node_is_allowed`).
- `obj->funcs->open()`: Backend callback.
- Finally, `idr_replace()` swaps `NULL` with the actual object pointer. This sequence prevents concurrent threads from looking up a half-initialized handle.

### Code Flow 1: Creating a Dumb Buffer
Let us trace GEM through the simplest buffer creation path: the <b>dumb buffer</b>. This is the most basic buffer used for software rendering on screen; it lacks GPU acceleration features and is essentially "flat memory that the CPU can paint into." Userspace creates one using a single ioctl: `DRM_IOCTL_MODE_CREATE_DUMB`.  

```mermaid
{
    "title": "DRM_IOCTL_MODE_CREATE_DUMB processing flow (shmem backend)"
}
---
sequenceDiagram
    participant U as Userspace
    participant D as drm_mode_create_dumb
    participant S as shmem helper
    participant G as GEM core
    U->>D: CREATE_DUMB<br/>(width, height, bpp)
    D->>D: Validate size<br/>(overflow, PAGE_ALIGN)
    D->>S: driver->dumb_create()
    S->>S: Create shmem file<br/>Reserve mmap offset
    S->>G: drm_gem_handle_create()
    G->>G: idr_alloc → handle
    G-->>U: handle · pitch · size
```
Calculating and validating the buffer size using `width`, `height`, and `bpp` passed by userspace is the core's job.  

```c
{
    "title": "drivers/gpu/drm/drm_dumb_buffers.c - drm_mode_create_dumb() (L191-228)"
}
---
int drm_mode_create_dumb(struct drm_device *dev,
			 struct drm_mode_create_dumb *args,
			 struct drm_file *file_priv)
{
	u32 cpp, stride, size;

	if (!dev->driver->dumb_create)  // [!code highlight]
		return -ENOSYS;
	if (!args->width || !args->height || !args->bpp)
		return -EINVAL;

	if (args->bpp > U32_MAX - 8)
		return -EINVAL;
	cpp = DIV_ROUND_UP(args->bpp, 8);
	if (cpp > U32_MAX / args->width)
		return -EINVAL;
	stride = cpp * args->width;
	if (args->height > U32_MAX / stride)
		return -EINVAL;

	size = args->height * stride;
	if (PAGE_ALIGN(size) == 0)  // [!code highlight]
		return -EINVAL;

	args->handle = 0;
	args->pitch = 0;
	args->size = 0;

	return dev->driver->dumb_create(file_priv, dev, args);  // [!code highlight]
}
```

> **Source** <a href="https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/v6.19/drivers/gpu/drm/drm_dumb_buffers.c#L191-L228" target="_blank">drivers/gpu/drm/drm_dumb_buffers.c L191-L228</a> (Linux v6.19)

`dev->driver->dumb_create` is the <b>driver callback</b>. As seen in Part 2, the `DRM_GEM_SHMEM_DRIVER_OPS` macro in vkms's `drm_driver` populates this callback with `drm_gem_shmem_dumb_create`.  

```c
{
    "title": "include/drm/drm_gem_shmem_helper.h - #define DRM_GEM_SHMEM_DRIVER_OPS (L302-304)"
}
---
#define DRM_GEM_SHMEM_DRIVER_OPS \
	.gem_prime_import       = drm_gem_shmem_prime_import_no_map, \
	.dumb_create            = drm_gem_shmem_dumb_create  // [!code highlight]
```

> **Source** <a href="https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/v6.19/include/drm/drm_gem_shmem_helper.h#L302-L304" target="_blank">include/drm/drm_gem_shmem_helper.h L302-L304</a> (Linux v6.19)


```adcard
```

```c
{
    "title": "drivers/gpu/drm/drm_gem_shmem_helper.c - drm_gem_shmem_dumb_create() (L558-568)"
}
---
int drm_gem_shmem_dumb_create(struct drm_file *file, struct drm_device *dev,
			      struct drm_mode_create_dumb *args)
{
	int ret;

	ret = drm_mode_size_dumb(dev, args, 0, 0);
	if (ret)
		return ret;

	return drm_gem_shmem_create_with_handle(file, dev, args->size, &args->handle);
}
```

> **Source** <a href="https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/v6.19/drivers/gpu/drm/drm_gem_shmem_helper.c#L558-L568" target="_blank">drivers/gpu/drm/drm_gem_shmem_helper.c L558-L568</a> (Linux v6.19)

```c
{
    "title": "drivers/gpu/drm/drm_gem_shmem_helper.c - drm_gem_shmem_create_with_handle() (L470-491)"
}
---
static int
drm_gem_shmem_create_with_handle(struct drm_file *file_priv,  // [!code highlight]
				 struct drm_device *dev, size_t size,
				 uint32_t *handle)
{
	struct drm_gem_shmem_object *shmem;
	int ret;

	shmem = drm_gem_shmem_create(dev, size);  // [!code highlight]
	if (IS_ERR(shmem))
		return PTR_ERR(shmem);

	ret = drm_gem_handle_create(file_priv, &shmem->base, handle);  // [!code highlight]

	drm_gem_object_put(&shmem->base);  // [!code highlight]

	return ret;
}
```

> **Source** <a href="https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/v6.19/drivers/gpu/drm/drm_gem_shmem_helper.c#L470-L491" target="_blank">drivers/gpu/drm/drm_gem_shmem_helper.c L470-L491</a> (Linux v6.19)

The operational sequence unfolds as follows:  

1. `drm_gem_shmem_create()` <b>creates</b> the object with an initial reference count of 1.
2. `drm_gem_handle_create()` creates the handle. Internally, `drm_gem_object_handle_get()` increments `handle_count`, taking an additional object reference <b>only when it is the first handle</b> (`if (obj->handle_count++ == 0) drm_gem_object_get(obj)`). Subsequent handles simply increment the counter.
3. Finally, `drm_gem_object_put()` <mark>drops the initial reference</mark> (source comment: "handle holds it now"). The object's lifecycle is now tied to the handle. When userspace closes the handle, or when the file is closed, `drm_gem_release()` releases all remaining handles in that file's `object_idr`, freeing the object.

Cleanup upon file closure is just as straightforward:  

```c
{
    "title": "drivers/gpu/drm/drm_gem.c - drm_gem_release() (L1043-1049)"
}
---
void
drm_gem_release(struct drm_device *dev, struct drm_file *file_private)
{
	idr_for_each(&file_private->object_idr,  // [!code highlight]
		     &drm_gem_object_release_handle, file_private);
	idr_destroy(&file_private->object_idr);  // [!code highlight]
}
```

> **Source** <a href="https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/v6.19/drivers/gpu/drm/drm_gem.c#L1043-L1049" target="_blank">drivers/gpu/drm/drm_gem.c L1043-L1049</a> (Linux v6.19)

Even if a process crashes unexpectedly, the kernel invokes this function while closing the fd, preventing GPU memory leaks. Now let us turn back to `__drm_gem_shmem_init()`, which creates the object and performs two critical tasks.  

```c
{
    "title": "drivers/gpu/drm/drm_gem_shmem_helper.c - __drm_gem_shmem_init() (L52-94)"
}
---
static int __drm_gem_shmem_init(struct drm_device *dev, struct drm_gem_shmem_object *shmem,
				size_t size, bool private, struct vfsmount *gemfs)
{
	struct drm_gem_object *obj = &shmem->base;
	int ret = 0;

	if (!obj->funcs)
		obj->funcs = &drm_gem_shmem_funcs;  // [!code highlight]

	if (private) {
		drm_gem_private_object_init(dev, obj, size);
		shmem->map_wc = false;
	} else {
		ret = drm_gem_object_init_with_mnt(dev, obj, size, gemfs);  // [!code highlight]
	}
	if (ret) {
		drm_gem_private_object_fini(obj);
		return ret;
	}

	ret = drm_gem_create_mmap_offset(obj);  // [!code highlight]
	if (ret)
		goto err_release;

	INIT_LIST_HEAD(&shmem->madv_list);

	if (!private) {
		mapping_set_gfp_mask(obj->filp->f_mapping, GFP_HIGHUSER |
				     __GFP_RETRY_MAYFAIL | __GFP_NOWARN);
	}

	return 0;
err_release:
	drm_gem_object_release(obj);
	return ret;
}
```

> **Source** <a href="https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/v6.19/drivers/gpu/drm/drm_gem_shmem_helper.c#L52-L94" target="_blank">drivers/gpu/drm/drm_gem_shmem_helper.c L52-L94</a> (Linux v6.19)

- `drm_gem_object_init_with_mnt()` creates a shmem file using <mark>shmem_file_setup("drm mm object", size, ...)</mark>. Each object is essentially an <b>anonymous memory file with a fixed size</b>.
- `drm_gem_create_mmap_offset()` pre-allocates and reserves the fake mmap offset for this object (covered in the mmap section below).

At this stage, <b>no physical pages have been allocated yet</b>. Pages are allocated on demand later inside `drm_gem_shmem_get_pages_locked()`.  

```c
{
    "title": "drivers/gpu/drm/drm_gem_shmem_helper.c - drm_gem_shmem_get_pages_locked() (L230-262)"
}
---
static int drm_gem_shmem_get_pages_locked(struct drm_gem_shmem_object *shmem)
{
	struct drm_gem_object *obj = &shmem->base;
	struct page **pages;

	dma_resv_assert_held(shmem->base.resv);

	if (refcount_inc_not_zero(&shmem->pages_use_count))  // [!code highlight]
		return 0;

	pages = drm_gem_get_pages(obj);  // [!code highlight]
	if (IS_ERR(pages)) {
		drm_dbg_kms(obj->dev, "Failed to get pages (%ld)\n",
			    PTR_ERR(pages));
		return PTR_ERR(pages);
	}

#ifdef CONFIG_X86
	if (shmem->map_wc)
		set_pages_array_wc(pages, obj->size >> PAGE_SHIFT);
#endif

	shmem->pages = pages;

	refcount_set(&shmem->pages_use_count, 1);  // [!code highlight]

	return 0;
}
```

> **Source** <a href="https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/v6.19/drivers/gpu/drm/drm_gem_shmem_helper.c#L230-L262" target="_blank">drivers/gpu/drm/drm_gem_shmem_helper.c L230-L262</a> (Linux v6.19)

```c
{
    "title": "drivers/gpu/drm/drm_gem.c - drm_gem_get_pages() (L623-688)"
}
---
struct page **drm_gem_get_pages(struct drm_gem_object *obj)
{
	/* ... */
	mapping = obj->filp->f_mapping;

	WARN_ON((obj->size & (PAGE_SIZE - 1)) != 0);

	npages = obj->size >> PAGE_SHIFT;
	/* ... */
	pages = kvmalloc_array(npages, sizeof(struct page *), GFP_KERNEL);
	/* ... */
	i = 0;
	while (i < npages) {
		unsigned long nr;
		folio = shmem_read_folio_gfp(mapping, i,  // [!code highlight]
				mapping_gfp_mask(mapping));
		if (IS_ERR(folio))
			goto fail;
		nr = min(npages - i, folio_nr_pages(folio));
		for (j = 0; j < nr; j++, i++)
			pages[i] = folio_file_page(folio, i);

		BUG_ON(mapping_gfp_constraint(mapping, __GFP_DMA32) &&
				(folio_pfn(folio) >= 0x00100000UL));
	}

	return pages;
	/* ... */
}
```

> **Source** <a href="https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/v6.19/drivers/gpu/drm/drm_gem.c#L623-L688" target="_blank">drivers/gpu/drm/drm_gem.c L623-L688</a> (Linux v6.19)

`shmem_read_folio_gfp()` reads pages from the shmem file (allocating them if not already present) into an array, and `mapping_set_unevictable()` <b>pins them to prevent swapping</b>. This is the concrete code implementing the principle stated earlier: "to be used by a GPU, memory must be pinned to physical pages."  

### Code Flow 2: Painting from the CPU with mmap
Although the buffer has been created, the CPU cannot access it yet. Because multiple buffers reside behind the single `/dev/dri/card0` file descriptor, calling `mmap(fd, ...)` alone <b>does not identify which buffer to map</b>. DRM solves this using a <mark>fake offset</mark>, repurposing the file offset (the final argument to `mmap`) as an object identifier.  

```mermaid
{
    "title": "Two stages of mmap: obtaining offset → mmap"
}
---
sequenceDiagram
    participant U as Userspace
    participant K as DRM core
    participant V as VM (page fault)
    U->>K: ioctl(MAP_DUMB, {handle})
    K->>K: handle → drm_gem_object (object_idr)
    K-->>U: offset (address of obj->vma_node)
    U->>K: mmap(NULL, size, ..., fd, offset)
    K->>K: drm_gem_mmap(): offset → vma_node → obj<br/>Check drm_vma_node_is_allowed()
    K->>K: drm_gem_mmap_obj(): setup vm_ops, funcs->mmap()
    U->>V: First memory access
    V->>K: Fault handler drm_gem_shmem_fault()
    K-->>V: Map shmem page into PTE
```


```adcard
```

#### Step 1: Getting the Offset (MAP_DUMB)
```c
{
    "title": "drivers/gpu/drm/drm_gem.c - drm_gem_dumb_map_offset() (L395-420)"
}
---
int drm_gem_dumb_map_offset(struct drm_file *file, struct drm_device *dev,
			    u32 handle, u64 *offset)
{
	struct drm_gem_object *obj;
	int ret;

	obj = drm_gem_object_lookup(file, handle);  // [!code highlight]
	if (!obj)
		return -ENOENT;

	if (drm_gem_is_imported(obj)) {
		ret = -EINVAL;
		goto out;
	}

	ret = drm_gem_create_mmap_offset(obj);  // [!code highlight]
	if (ret)
		goto out;

	*offset = drm_vma_node_offset_addr(&obj->vma_node);  // [!code highlight]
out:
	drm_gem_object_put(obj);

	return ret;
}
```

> **Source** <a href="https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/v6.19/drivers/gpu/drm/drm_gem.c#L395-L420" target="_blank">drivers/gpu/drm/drm_gem.c L395-L420</a> (Linux v6.19)

`drm_gem_object_lookup()` looks up the object using the handle (`object_idr` query) and returns the address of the reserved `vma_node` as the offset. This offset is <b>not an actual file offset, but a fake address managed per device by `drm_vma_offset_manager`</b>.  

```c
{
    "title": "drivers/gpu/drm/drm_gem.c - drm_gem_create_mmap_offset_size() (L557-564)"
}
---
int
drm_gem_create_mmap_offset_size(struct drm_gem_object *obj, size_t size)
{
	struct drm_device *dev = obj->dev;

	return drm_vma_offset_add(dev->vma_offset_manager, &obj->vma_node,  // [!code highlight]
				  size / PAGE_SIZE);
}
```

> **Source** <a href="https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/v6.19/drivers/gpu/drm/drm_gem.c#L557-L564" target="_blank">drivers/gpu/drm/drm_gem.c L557-L564</a> (Linux v6.19)

#### Step 2: mmap()
The `mmap()` syscall enters via <mark>.mmap = drm_gem_mmap</mark> in `DRM_GEM_FOPS`, which we explored in Part 3.  

```c
{
    "title": "drivers/gpu/drm/drm_gem.c - drm_gem_mmap() (L1205-1251)"
}
