### Summary
In this post, we have explored the following:  

- GPU buffers are represented by a single <b>GEM object</b>, which userspace references via an <b>independent handle per fd (drm_file)</b>. The handle's lifetime governs the object's lifetime.
- Dumb buffers are implemented through a <b>shmem file + mmap fake offset</b>, with physical pages allocated on demand when first needed.
- `mmap` resolves the object via the fake offset and maps backing pages one by one upon a <b>page fault</b>. `drm_vma_node_is_allowed()` prevents unauthorized processes from mapping arbitrary buffers by simply knowing their offset.

Handles are meaningful only within their originating `drm_file`. Next, we will explore how to <b>share these buffers with other processes or hardware devices</b> using PRIME and dma-buf.
